<p>
<a href="https://quad9.net/">Quad9</a> is a free DNS resolver that offers privacy and security. They offer privacy by not keeping any logs about who is using them to look up what. They offer security by maintaining a list of malware sites and blocking them.
</p>
<p>
Now they are leaving France because the French courts are ordering them to block certain sports-based pirate sites. Quad9 points out that they have no way of knowing who or where a given user is so they can only comply by blocking the sites for everyone worldwide. The French courts refused to hear it so Quad9 is reluctantly leaving France.
</p>
<p>
Over at <a href="https://techstackups.com">Tech Stackups</a>, Al Smith has a <a href="https://techstackups.com/articles/quad9-leaves-france-dns-blocking/">thorough analysis of the case</a> and argues that DNS is the wrong place to fight piracy and that forcing Quad9 out of the country only serves to deprive the French people of a free, useful, and secure DNS resolver without hurting the pirates at all.
</p>
<p>
Perhaps when the Internet suddenly become inaccessible to all those using Quad9—including some government agencies—the courts might become willing to listen. As Quad9 puts it, how would France react if some country on the other side of the globe forced the blocking of sites that its people used. That’s exactly what France is doing.
</p>
<p>
This sort of thing never ends well, of course, and when people all over the world—including France—start suffering the consequences, it will somehow be “Big Tech’s” fault.</p>
